{"sections":[],"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"title":"Styling","role":"article","roleHeading":"Article"},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"Overview","level":2,"text":"Overview","type":"heading"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Every default placeholder ships two independent levers: a "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"style","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (how big a footprint it expects to own) and, in SwiftUI, a "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"theme","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (what color\/font it uses). On top of the default spinner, two alternative Loading placeholders cover the other common shapes a loading state takes: a pulsing skeleton, and a redacted mirror of the real content."}]},{"anchor":"compact-style","level":2,"text":"`.compact` style","type":"heading"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatePlaceholderStyle","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" controls how much space a default placeholder expects to fill. Every default placeholder’s initializer takes a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"style:"},{"type":"text","text":" parameter, defaulting to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".standard"},{"type":"text","text":" — the full-screen treatment: a full-size icon, and, for the Loading spinner, filling all available space."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Pass "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".compact"},{"type":"text","text":" to embed the same placeholder in a card or section instead of a whole screen: a smaller icon, smaller text, and — for the Loading spinner — no more forcing itself to fill the available space:"}]},{"syntax":"swift","code":["ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView(style: .compact)","ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView(style: .compact)"],"type":"codeListing"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":".compact","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" always keeps a vertical icon-above-text layout, regardless of the device’s orientation — a card’s size is dictated by the card, not the device, unlike the automatic landscape-iPhone icon-beside-text switch ","type":"text"},{"code":".standard","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" makes on its own."}]},{"text":"Alternative Loading placeholders","type":"heading","anchor":"Alternative-Loading-placeholders","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" — the spinner — are what every default-wired "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateView"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"ScreenStateContainerView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"ScreenStateViewController","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" shows. Two opt-in alternatives cover the other common shapes a loading state takes:","type":"text"}]},{"items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateShimmerLoadingView","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateShimmerLoadingUIView","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":": a pulsing skeleton of a circular avatar plus two text-line placeholders, for a generic “something’s coming” look that doesn’t need to know the real content’s shape."}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateRedactedLoadingView","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":": renders your real content view through SwiftUI’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".redacted(reason: .placeholder)"},{"type":"text","text":", so the placeholder mirrors the exact shape of the content it’s standing in for. SwiftUI-only — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"redacted(reason:)"},{"type":"text","text":" has no UIKit equivalent."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"type":"unorderedList"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["ScreenStateShimmerLoadingView()","","ScreenStateRedactedLoadingView {","    ArticleRow(article: .placeholder)","}"],"type":"codeListing"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Both respect Reduce Motion and Increase Contrast the same way the default spinner does; see ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"text":"Theming","type":"heading","anchor":"Theming","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatesTheme","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" lets a SwiftUI app theme every default placeholder at once — ","type":"text"},{"code":"tint","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"titleFont","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and ","type":"text"},{"code":"messageFont","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — instead of passing a fully custom placeholder view at every call site. Set it once via ","type":"text"},{"code":"View.screenStatesTheme(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" on a container view, and every default placeholder beneath it in the view hierarchy (including ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateShimmerLoadingView","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":") picks it up:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","code":["MyScreenView()","    .screenStatesTheme(ScreenStatesTheme(tint: .indigo, titleFont: .title2, messageFont: .callout))"],"type":"codeListing"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Any property left ","type":"text"},{"code":"nil","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (the default, via ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatesTheme\/standard","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":") keeps the built-in gradient\/font. Increase Contrast still wins over ","type":"text"},{"code":"tint","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" regardless — it’s an accessibility need, not a branding preference.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"There’s no UIKit equivalent: UIKit has no concept of an inherited environment, so the UIKit default placeholders keep taking their configuration directly through their initializers.","type":"text"}]}]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Styling","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Related-Documentation","title":"Related Documentation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorView","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility"]},{"anchor":"Placeholder-styling","generated":true,"title":"Placeholder styling","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatePlaceholderStyle","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatesTheme"]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/styling"]}],"abstract":[{"text":"The built-in style variants, alternative Loading placeholders, and the theming configuration point — everything beyond the out-of-the-box defaults for tuning